  "source": "Cox & D'Antonio 3e Table 6.2, PDF page 250 (printed p. 193)",
  "about": "Rendered PDF page 250, folio 193 (running head \"Porous sound absorption 193\"); the whole page is the table. Title as printed: \"Table 6.2 Examples of flow resistivities reported in various acoustic studies\". Two columns, headings in italic: \"Material\" and \"Flow resistivity (rayl m-1)\" (the -1 is a superscript). Rows sit under four bold section headings, \"Fibrous materials\", \"Cellular materials\", \"Granular materials\" and \"Other\", kept here as the group field. No footnotes. Material names carry superscript reference numbers into the chapter 6 reference list (PDF pages 276-281, printed pp. 219-224); \"Mineral wool\" carries none. Section 6.4.1 on the facing page (PDF 249, printed p. 192) introduces the table with \"Table 6.2 gives some example values (for ground, see Table 6.7)\", after stating that \"The unit of flow resistance in MKS units is N m−3 s, often referred to as 1 rayl, and should nowadays be used. Older texts may use CGS rayls. To convert a CGS rayl to a MKS rayl, multiply by 10.\" The printed unit rayl m-1 (MKS rayl per metre) is therefore N s m-4 = Pa s m-2, so every number is carried over with factor 1; thousands separators dropped. Ranges printed with an en dash go to ranges; the one cell printing two values joined by \"and\" (glass beads) keeps both readings in one row, unpaired, because the page does not say which value belongs to which bead size. The prose gives no per-material caveats; the title itself says the values are examples reported in studies, not specifications. Odd: pairing the two by printed order would give the larger glass bead (1.68 mm) the larger flow resistivity (43,200), the opposite of the usual trend with grain size, and Table 6.5 prints the same pair from the same study with the sizes reversed; neither is paired here.",
